  "critiquePairs": [
    {
      "id": "cp-1",
      "left": "enum-switch",
      "right": "strong",
      "question": "A new promotion type arrives every week. Which design absorbs that by adding a class rather than editing one? Click the abstraction that makes it possible.",
      "targetsConcept": "open-closed",
      "answer": {
        "design": "strong",
        "className": "Promotion"
      },
      "telling": "On the right, a promotion is an implementation of Promotion; a new one is a new class and the engine never changes. On the left, DiscountType is an enum and calculateTotal switches on it, so every new promotion is a new case in a method the whole cart depends on. The brief warned that the rules are written by people who do not deploy code — an enum is a deploy."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp-2",
      "left": "strong",
      "right": "rules-know-each-other",
      "question": "Two promotions must never stack. In which design is that decided in exactly one place? Click it.",
      "targetsConcept": "cohesion",
      "answer": {
        "design": "strong",
        "className": "CombinationPolicy"
      },
      "telling": "On the left, exclusivity is a CombinationPolicy — one class that sees all candidates and decides. On the right, each promotion lists what it conflicts with, so adding a promotion means editing every promotion it must not combine with, and two rules can disagree about each other. Knowledge that is about pairs belongs in one place that sees pairs."
    }
  ],
